N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit: bar 1 call G-G-A-G (four eighths); bar 2 sung answer E-E-G (two eighths and a quarter); bar 3 call G-G-A-A (four eighths), ending open on la; bar 4 two quarter rests, the first percussion answer; bar 5 call A-G-A-G (four eighths); bar 6 two quarter rests, the second percussion answer; bar 7 everyone E-G-A-G (four eighths); bar 8 E4 quarter and a quarter rest, the close on mi. Every bar totals two quarter beats; the only pitches are E4, G4 and A4; range E4-A4; the largest leap is the minor third between E and G; durations are eighths, quarters and quarter rests only. The calls rise toward la and the answers settle on mi, the resting tone of a mi-so-la song. Originality: the eighth-note so-so-la-so call is a common mi-so-la playground shape; the tune was checked against "Colombia Tierra Querida", "Rain, Rain, Go Away", "Lucy Locket" and "Bounce High, Bounce Low" and shares no phrase with them.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 1 percussion groups can enter and stop cleanly in the one-bar answers at 104, whether the Band's march groove helps or crowds the silent bars, and which Bermúdez recording a teacher should play are untested or unsettled: Wikipedia dates the song's introduction to a 1970 CBS album with Matilde Díaz, an anthology of 1946-1961 coastal recordings also carries it, and the linked Sony upload credits ℗ 1972 CBS. A Colombian music educator, a classroom trial and a second score reader remain required.
